**Operator overloading (C++).** Giving an operator a user-defined meaning for a class, resolved at compile time. It is syntactic sugar for a function call.

```cpp
class Complex {
    double re, im;
public:
    Complex(double r = 0, double i = 0) : re(r), im(i) {}
    Complex operator+(const Complex& o) const { return Complex(re + o.re, im + o.im); }
};
Complex a(1, 2), b(3, 4);
Complex c = a + b;   // calls a.operator+(b)
```

Operators that cannot be overloaded include `::`, `.`, `.*`, `?:` and `sizeof`.

**Function overriding.** A derived class provides its own implementation of a `virtual` function declared in the base class with the same signature. The call is resolved at **runtime** through the vtable when invoked through a base pointer or reference.

| | Operator/function overloading | Overriding |
|---|---|---|
| Scope | Same class (or namespace) | Base and derived class |
| Signature | Must differ | Must be the same |
| Binding | Compile time | Runtime (needs `virtual`) |

**Friend function.** A non-member function declared with `friend` inside a class gets access to its private and protected members. It is not a member and has no `this`. Typical use: `operator<<` for printing an object, or functions that need access to two classes.

```cpp
class Box { int w; friend void show(const Box&); public: Box(int x):w(x){} };
void show(const Box& b) { std::cout << b.w; }
```

It weakens encapsulation, so use it sparingly; friendship is not inherited and is not mutual.

**Header files in C.** A header (`.h`) holds declarations: function prototypes, macros, `typedef`s and `struct` definitions, shared across source files. `#include <stdio.h>` makes the preprocessor paste its text before compilation, so the compiler knows the prototypes; the actual code is linked from libraries. Use include guards (`#ifndef` / `#define` / `#endif`) or `#pragma once` to prevent double inclusion. Angle brackets search system paths; quotes search the local directory first.
